  "activity": {
    "id": "dna_structure_and_replication_activity",
    "name": "DNA Structure and Replication Lab Experience",
    "description": "An interactive AR lab exploring the three-dimensional structure of DNA and the semiconservative replication process. Students examine the double helix components, identify base pairing rules, learn the antiparallel orientation, and trace the action of key replication enzymes at the replication fork.",
    "workplace": "dna_replication_lab",
    "start": "step_1_dna_overview",
    "actions": [
      {
        "id": "step_1_dna_overview",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "Introduction to DNA Structure",
          "description": "Welcome to the DNA Structure and Replication Lab. You will explore the three-dimensional double helix molecule. Start by observing the complete DNA double helix model. Notice the two sugar-phosphate backbones spiraling around a central axis, and the nitrogenous bases projecting inward. Listen to the narration that describes the overall architecture of DNA."
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "dna_helix_model",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "dna_double_helix",
              "poi": "default"
            },
            {
              "target": "lab_station_one",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "narration_dna_structure",
              "poi": "default"
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": []
        },
        "exit": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "dna_helix_model",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "antiparallel_strands_label",
              "poi": "sugar_phosphate_backbone_left"
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": []
        },
        "triggers": [
          {
            "mode": "click",
            "id": "proceed_button_1"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "step_2_base_pairing",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "Complementary Base Pairing",
          "description": "Now focus on the bases within the helix. DNA contains four nitrogenous bases: adenine (A), thymine (T), guanine (G), and cytosine (C). These bases pair specifically \u2014 adenine pairs with thymine via two hydrogen bonds, and guanine pairs with cytosine via three hydrogen bonds. Examine the detailed base pair models and the diagram showing hydrogen bond networks. This complementary pairing ensures accurate replication and stability of the double helix."
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "dna_helix_model",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "base_pair_adenine_thymine",
              "poi": "base_pair_region"
            },
            {
              "target": "lab_station_one",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "hydrogen_bonds_diagram",
              "poi": "display_screen"
            },
            {
              "target": "lab_station_one",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "narration_base_pairing",
              "poi": "default"
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": []
        },
        "exit": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "dna_helix_model",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "base_pair_guanine_cytosine",
              "poi": "base_pair_region"
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": []
        },
        "triggers": [
          {
            "mode": "click",
            "id": "proceed_button_2"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "step_3_antiparallel_directionality",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "Antiparallel Orientation and 5'-to-3' Directionality",
          "description": "The two strands of DNA run in opposite directions \u2014 one strand from 5' to 3' and the complementary strand from 3' to 5'. This antiparallel arrangement is a consequence of how the sugar-phosphate backbone is chemically oriented. The 5' end of the sugar has a phosphate group; the 3' end has a hydroxyl group. This directionality is critical for DNA replication, because DNA polymerase can only synthesize new DNA in the 5'-to-3' direction. Observe how the two backbones spiral in opposite orientations."
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "dna_helix_model",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "dna_double_helix",
              "poi": "sugar_phosphate_backbone_left"
            },
            {
              "target": "dna_helix_model",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "dna_double_helix",
              "poi": "sugar_phosphate_backbone_right"
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": []
        },
        "exit": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [],
          "deactivate": [
            {
              "target": "dna_helix_model",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "*"
            }
          ]
        },
        "triggers": [
          {
            "mode": "click",
            "id": "proceed_button_3"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "step_4_replication_enzymes",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "Enzymes of DNA Replication",
          "description": "DNA replication is carried out by several key enzymes. Helicase unwinds the double helix at the replication fork, breaking hydrogen bonds between base pairs. Single-strand binding proteins (SSB) stabilize the exposed single strands, preventing them from re-annealing. DNA polymerase synthesizes new DNA strands by adding nucleotides to the 3'-OH group, using each parental strand as a template. Watch as these enzymes work together at the replication fork. Pay attention to which enzyme performs each function."
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "replication_fork_model",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "helicase_enzyme",
              "poi": "unwinding_region"
            },
            {
              "target": "replication_fork_model",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "ssb_proteins",
              "poi": "unwinding_region"
            },
            {
              "target": "lab_station_two",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "narration_replication_enzymes",
              "poi": "default"
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": []
        },
        "exit": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "replication_fork_model",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "replication_fork_animation",
              "poi": "default"
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": []
        },
        "triggers": [
          {
            "mode": "click",
            "id": "proceed_button_4"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "step_5_leading_lagging_strands",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "Leading and Lagging Strand Synthesis",
          "description": "Because DNA polymerase can only extend DNA in the 5'-to-3' direction, and because the two template strands are antiparallel, the two new strands are synthesized differently. The leading strand is synthesized continuously in the 5'-to-3' direction, following the replication fork. The lagging strand is synthesized discontinuously in short segments called Okazaki fragments, each made in the 5'-to-3' direction, but overall running opposite to the replication fork direction. DNA ligase then joins these fragments together. Examine the diagram and animation showing how this asymmetry arises from the fundamental constraints of DNA structure and polymerase directionality."
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "replication_fork_model",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "dna_polymerase_enzyme",
              "poi": "leading_strand_synthesis"
            },
            {
              "target": "replication_fork_model",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "dna_polymerase_enzyme",
              "poi": "lagging_strand_synthesis"
            },
            {
              "target": "lab_station_two",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "leading_lagging_diagram",
              "poi": "enzyme_display"
            },
            {
              "target": "replication_fork_model",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "okazaki_fragments_animation",
              "poi": "default"
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": []
        },
        "exit": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "replication_fork_model",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "dna_ligase_enzyme",
              "poi": "lagging_strand_synthesis"
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": []
        },
        "triggers": [
          {
            "mode": "click",
            "id": "proceed_button_5"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "step_6_semiconservative_replication",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "Semiconservative Replication and Daughter Molecules",
          "description": "DNA replication is described as semiconservative because each daughter DNA molecule consists of one original (parental) strand and one newly-synthesized strand. This was demonstrated in the Meselson-Stahl experiment using isotope labeling. After one round of replication, both daughter molecules are hybrids containing one heavy (original) strand and one light (new) strand. After a second round of replication, one daughter contains both original strands (hybrid), and one contains only new strands. Examine the diagram showing the composition of daughter molecules, and understand why semiconservative replication is essential for accurate transmission of genetic information."
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "daughter_dna_molecules",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "dna_double_helix",
              "poi": "molecule_1"
            },
            {
              "target": "daughter_dna_molecules",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "dna_double_helix",
              "poi": "molecule_2"
            },
            {
              "target": "lab_station_two",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "semiconservative_replication_diagram",
              "poi": "display_screen"
            },
            {
              "target": "lab_station_two",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "narration_semiconservative",
              "poi": "default"
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": []
        },
        "exit": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [],
          "deactivate": [
            {
              "target": "*",
              "type": "*"
            }
          ]
        },
        "triggers": [
          {
            "mode": "click",
            "id": "conclude_button"
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
